// A `try` inside a `//!` prompt case, which was a live COMPILE-FAIL from
// ordinary source until tryBoundaryRefusal admitted the checker's SECOND
// spelling for a test activation.
//
// The checker writes `test %q` for a `test "name"` declaration and
// `attached test for <owner>` for a prompt case (testBoundaryOverride), and both
// are one activation: the case aborts and the run continues. Matching only the
// first classified this `try` as a FUNCTION boundary, so tryGuard looked at
// g.result — kindInvalid, because a test body declares no result — and
// SUPPRESSED rather than refused. A suppression records no refusal, so Generate
// had nothing to discard the lowering for, and the file went out with the guard
// missing and its binding unread: `declared and not used`.
//
// Why the guards already in place could not see it. The corpus reads 0
// COMPILE-FAIL because no corpus file has a `try` in a prompt case, and
// TestIRBuild_EveryEmittedFileParsesAsGo cannot see a TYPE error — an unused local
// parses perfectly well as Go. So this is a differential fixture, which builds
// what it emits.
//
// What each case discriminates:
//
//   - `propagating` — the prompt PROPAGATES. The case must FAIL, and its report
//     is an EarlyReturnFailure carrying the `try`'s own line, the expression as
//     the formatter renders it, and the propagated variant as value.Inspect
//     renders it. A guard emitted at the wrong boundary either fails to compile
//     (the shape above) or, worse, returns from something that is not the case.
//   - `passing` — the same shape with an `Ok`, so the binding is read and the
//     prompt passes. Without it a lowering that always exited early would pass
//     the fixture's failing half.
//   - the file-scope `test` — an ordinary `test "name"` boundary beside the
//     prompt ones, so the two spellings are exercised in one program.
//! v = try parse(1)
//! assert v == 1
//
fn passing(n: Int): Int {
    n
}

//! bad = try parse(-1)
//! assert bad == 0
//
fn propagating(n: Int): Int {
    n
}

fn parse(n: Int): Result<Int, String> {
    if n < 0 { Err("negative") } else { Ok(n) }
}

test "an ordinary test declaration is the other test boundary spelling" {
    ok = try parse(2)
    assert ok == 2
}
